1://! The WebM writer (spec §11 CX-6, CX-15): one file of AV1 video and Opus
2://! sound, written from compressed packets the app hands in as opaque bytes.
3://! Nothing here encodes or decodes; it decides the container — the header,
4://! the timestamps, the interleave, the Clusters and the Cues.
5://!
6://! **The layout** (CX-15 (3)) is fixed at `open` from a [`WebmLayout`]: the
7://! EBML header (DocType `webm` 4/2), a Segment whose size is patched at
8://! `finish`, a Void reserved for the SeekHead `finish` writes over it, Info,
9://! Tracks and, when there are any, Chapters. Clusters follow as packets
10://! arrive, and Cues close the file. Element bytes are `webm_ebml.rs`'s.
11://!
12://! **The interleave** (CX-15 (4)): each declared track has a queue, and the
13://! earliest head (by its millisecond time, video first among equal times) is
14://! written only while every declared track that has not ended has a head,
15://! so the file's blocks are in nondecreasing time whatever order two pumps
16://! deliver them in. `end_track` releases a track's claim; a layout with one
17://! track writes each packet as it arrives. Each call ends with a flush, so
18://! what was written is on disk when it returns.
19://!
20://! **Clusters** start before each video key block, and before a block whose
21://! time would pass the 16-bit relative limit (32,767 ms); a Cluster's size is
22://! written unknown and patched when the next one starts. Each key-started
23://! Cluster is a CuePoint.
